#{[17592186525038 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186524930 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525038 #edb/ref 1069 "q-166145" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525038 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525038 #edb/ref 1071 "Find ${h}^{′}(-2)$, if $h(x)=g(f(x)).$\n\n${h}^{′}(-2)\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525038 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c5c7d2a8-3068-5036-8a7c-19c736f09c46" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525038 #edb/ref 1074 "The chain rule gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)={g}^{′}(f(x))\\cdot {f}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=-2$ and using the values given in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(-2) & ={g}^{′}(f(-2))\\cdot {f}^{′}(-2) \\\\  & ={g}^{′}(2)\\cdot {f}^{′}(-2) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =48.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525043 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186524915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525043 #edb/ref 1069 "q-166147" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525043 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525043 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/7e/7e6d36041e7bd7fe1ecf7cae5f069f5714e06fb546151c7c5d1238219975e811.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=g(x)$ for $x\\in [-4,-1]$ is shown above. Complete the missing part in the statement below.\n\nThe mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c\\in (-4,-1)$ such that ${g}^{′}(c)\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525043 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "caea6f22-353c-51f3-b53e-c4874e2c30a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525043 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that $g(x)$ is continuous on the interval $[-4,-1]$ and differentiable on  $(-4,-1).$\n\nGiven that $g(x)$ is continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable on $(a,b)$, the mean value theorem states the following: \n\n> *There exists a point $c\\in (a,b)$ such that*\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(c)=\\frac{g(b)-g(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=-4$ and $b=-1.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{g(-1)-g(-4)}{-1-(-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-(-1)}{-1+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complete sentence is as follows:\n\n>  *The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c\\in (-4,-1)$ such that ${g}^{′}(c)=\\frac{2}{3}.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525048 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186524856 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525048 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186524891 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525048 #edb/ref 1069 "q-166152" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525048 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525048 #edb/ref 1071 "Write ${({r}^{-1})}^{′}(x)$ as a function of ${r}^{-1}(x).$\n\n$({r}^{-1})^{′}(x)\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}} \n$\\cdot (5{r}^{-1}(x)-$\n{{field-2}}\n$)^{2}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525048 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "da95b5d5-ad91-5dbb-9815-db7c7936c826" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525048 #edb/ref 1074 "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y=r(x)=\\frac{3}{5x-2},$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3}{5y-2}\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={r}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we need to differentiate with respect to $y$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$ In part 7, we found that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{15}(5y-2)^{2}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of ${r}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={r}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{r}^{-1}(x)] & =-\\frac{1}{15}{(5{r}^{-1}(x)-2)}^{2} \\\\ ({r}^{-1})^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{15}\\cdot {(5{r}^{-1}(x)-\\,2)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525065 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186524849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525065 #edb/ref 1069 "q-166149" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525065 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525065 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the vertical asymptotes of $y=r(x),$ where $r(x)=\\frac{3}{5x-2}.$\n\nThe vertical asymptote is $x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525065 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fd542e6d-453b-52cc-8c0f-6c5dc531d308" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525065 #edb/ref 1074 "First, recall that \n\n$$\ny=r(x)=\\frac{3}{5x-2}.\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator. So, we find the vertical asymptotes by setting the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n5x-2=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $x=\\frac{2}{5}$ is the only vertical asymptote of $y=r(x),$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/61/61d7b22d6df4c7cb85a814e84a8af2f8d162fe75d25b4e3e09258bfe965719ff.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525069 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186525143 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525069 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "045f6731-93bd-5066-8f29-40f1ddabb374" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525069 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186525065 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525089 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186525139 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525089 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3f90975a-f188-5c18-92df-9aa069bb827f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525089 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186524973 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525100 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6a4f162e-5f2f-506b-b3ad-81b4b2420a5f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525100 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186525048 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525103 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186525089 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525103 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "72ff08ee-a67b-566c-9514-7420dab45774" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525103 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186525038 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525112 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186525069 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525112 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8e3f066e-92e5-5bf6-a13b-617247ca6557" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525112 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186525013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525116 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186525103 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186525